in a constant struggle between his  American and Taiwanese backgrounds as a  child award-winning food author Frankie  gaw wanted nothing more than to fit in  with his classmates in the midwest but  as he figured out what it means to be  Asian-American he was caught between  homemade dumplings and Costco hot dogs  now in his debut cookbook first  generation God writes of family memories  in self-discovery while sharing recipes  rooted in Taiwan as well as those  influenced by the American culinary  dream we visited him in his home state  of Ohio  I usually do like size of a ping pong  ball  oh yeah that's like our family yeah he  and his mother continue the tradition  today Generations huddled around the  kitchen island what kind of dumpling  flavors did Grandma this one now the  food author is Sherry his family's  recipes for home style cooking along  with dishes influenced by his Midwestern  Roots what was it like growing up in  Ohio I basically grew up in my house as  a Taiwanese family but then outside is  very much my American upbringing even  when Gaul left eventually landing on the  west coast a sense of Home beckoned  the now 31 year old was at the top of  the tech industry in his 20s helping  design Facebook live before starting a  food blog its title little fat boy  inspired by loving family nickname I  knew I wanted to celebrate the food from  my family and do it in a way that almost  like I would approach it from like a  product perspective so to me the product  was this food that I grew up with that I  hadn't really seen that much in food  media  what began as a way to document his  family's recipes  [Music]  quickly found a following combining his  design and photography skills gauze  Showcase of Taiwanese home cooking once  a verse blog of the Year award in 2019.  so this is the dough this is the dose  it's not just ingredients that are  featured Once you pull it it'll divide  it so then you'll be able to pull it  apart but also technique that's great  see  this is too long no so you can you can  you can pull it apart here but the food  God now celebrates was once the source  of embarrassment  you used to be ashamed of these dishes  so much so that you would never bring  them to school yeah I wanted to very  much fit in within my American  upbringing and because I was one of the  only Asians like within school I already  stood out God describes emulating  classmates as a way of social survival  including when his dad sent him on a  camping trip was Taiwanese scallion  pancakes  but then as soon as you got on that trip  you threw it away yeah he made me a  batch of Pancakes that he was like you  can cook these at the campfire it would  be super cool and I just like tossed  them in the trash because I was like  there's no way I'm gonna cook these very  foreign scallion pancakes in front of  all my friends who aren't Asian it's  funny now because like I would want to  make that dish over and over for all my  friends today because it's just so  delicious instead his lunches were a  kaleidoscope of American junk food I'll  get gushers and fruit roll-ups and  Cinnamon Toast Crunch like these are all  in I went all in on like the Midwest  like uh Greatest Hits and that was my  identity and and I still love those  Foods just as much as I loved my  Taiwanese Foods they were I think at  that time just very much in their own  like different worlds and I never let  them kind of cross  generation features traditional  Taiwanese dishes like Ramen noodles  drizzled in a mix of ginger and garlic  peanut sauce alongside creative  innovations that reflect his childhood  gau's Chinese sausage corn dogs pay  homage to the Costco ones he'd microwave  after school this is a very distinct  flavor it has this interesting mix of  feeling like you're at a carnival in  Ohio and eating a dim sum so there's  also chili crisp and honey roasted  chicken yeah go for it and butternut  squash and pork dumplings what are you  making Poco alongside Pearl meatballs  like his grandmother made eating um rice  meatballs the rice is sticky sweet  glutinous rice The Filling is a pork and  cabbage very traditional filling with  Gingers and scallion  and this is delicious gauze Cincinnati  chili with hand pulled noodles are  topped with Parmesan cheese their  flavors cumin cinnamon and even  unsweetened chocolate the inspiration  one of Ohio's best known dishes Skyline  Chili this is one of Cincinnati's most  famous restaurants yeah this is a  Cincinnati staple they're known for  their chili our order the four-way thank  you very much ah look at that oh my gosh  there's so much cheese a lot of cheese  spaghetti topped with chili onions and  yes plenty of cheese this like became  our family's tradition I feel like one  of our first like truly American  traditions that we kind of adopted into  our own family you came to Skyline Chili  a lot with your dad yeah this is one of  his favorite places to go he always got  the chili spaghetti so I think that  would be called a two-way technically  without the cheese without the cheese he  was very lactic he couldn't eat the  cheese  God's cookbook is dedicated in part to  his father Ben who passed away from  cancer in 2014. his death pushed God to  question who he is and in the book he  calls himself his father's son he wrote  a letter to your dad in this book yeah  it's essentially my coming out letter to  him because I never got to come out to  him before he passed his passing was  almost the Catalyst for me to come out  because it was the jump start to a lot  of me figuring out my identity and so  this is kind of me just reflecting back  on my dad and the foods that he would  cook for me and me wanting to explain to  him where I'm at now in my life today if  I could talk to him still a  re-examination of food and family  God to embrace his identity and food he  writes has been at the heart of  discovering both deep shame and  overflowing Pride a lot of my asian-ness  was suppressed it's only been within the  last few years what I've really thought  through these recipes and these stories  and and taken the time to celebrate them  the  Crunch cereal got loved as a child  Cheers Cheers yeah  his Nostalgia focusing on the sweet in  the Bittersweet if there's anything that  you could tell  eight-year-old Frankie what would you  say oh  I would say don't Chuck this guy in  pancakes they're gonna be your career  one day  and I would just say hey in the end  no one's going to care as much as you  care so you should just be yourself and  because in the end it's not even going  to matter it's only how it matters to  you and where you want to go so I think  that's what I would say to myself  words to live by and we did bring that  Mochi that Frankie was talking about  here we do we actually have two sweet  treats made by our producer Emily de  Alessandro and yourself thanks Emily and  thank you she made the Mochi I also made  these Rice Krispie treats they are  oolong milk tea and almond Rice Krispie  treats and so you finally grind the  Oolong tea so that it really infuses all  of this it is my first time baking for  y'all so you got to tell me what you  think it's good I like it I had a little  bit thank you for doing that by the way  I know that's never baked for us and I  never will I  do like by the way I do like the cheers  the the dessert shears yes oh yeah all  right here cheers  there we go cheers
 

